WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
SIERRA: Hey, everyone. So, even though Why&nbsp;
It Matters is moving onto other topics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.160
there's still a lot of questions about COVID-19,&nbsp;
and a great place to find information is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.020
new site from the Council called ThinkGlobal&nbsp;
Health. It features some incredible reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.820
and they're doing a ton of fascinating work&nbsp;
on the pandemic. There's a lot of conflicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:26.400
information out there. And, along with the&nbsp;
CDC and WHO websites, ThinkGlobal Health is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.080
a really great way to get grounded and to&nbsp;
begin understanding what's going on. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.300
check it out - ah, thank ya!
﻿

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.920
ECONOMY: I will relate that interesting&nbsp;
story, the president of Kazakhstan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.680
actually visited a company called Hikvision.&nbsp;
It’s another one that provides surveillance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:53.220
technology. He visited their office in China.&nbsp;
And he saw how with one click on a person's face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.499
you could get that person's school history,&nbsp;
work history, financial situation -

00:00:59.499 --> 00:00:59.511
SIERRA: Wow.

00:00:59.511 --> 00:01:06.900
ECONOMY: And, wait for it, how did this&nbsp;
person spend his or her leisure time. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.320
where did this person go to have fun? Did&nbsp;
you go to the movies? Did you stop by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.740
bank? Did you go to the post office? Were you&nbsp;
hanging out with friends? Did you participate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.080
in a protest? And his reaction after seeing&nbsp;
all of this was, "we need this technology."

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.600
SIERRA: Oh. That's not where&nbsp;
I thought the story was going.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:28.200
SIERRA: This is probably not the first time you’re&nbsp;
hearing about China’s surveillance technology.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.820
And that’s because it gets a lot of&nbsp;
coverage. It’s like a Black-Mirror episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.760
it gives us visions of a dystopian future.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.800
But this technology, and the eagerness of&nbsp;
some countries to begin implementing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.580
is only a small part of a&nbsp;
much bigger story about China.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.820
Through its Belt and Road initiative, China is in&nbsp;
the process of building and funding infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:57.420
projects across the globe, and loaning vast sums&nbsp;
of money in the developing world. Some observers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.760
argue that as it does this, China is also&nbsp;
exporting its authoritarian model of government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.220
and eroding democratic norms that many of&nbsp;
us take for granted. Others say that China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.960
is simply taking business opportunities where&nbsp;
it sees them, and providing countries with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.920
alternative to a global order that&nbsp;
has gone unchallenged for decades.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.660
The debate comes down to one question&nbsp;
- how will we choose to view China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.920
as they pour money into hospitals,&nbsp;
ports, and roads around the world?

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:33.120
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It Matters.&nbsp;
Today, is China exporting authoritarianism?

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.840
ECONOMY: I think the most important&nbsp;
thing to understand about China's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.280
foreign policy over the past 10 years or&nbsp;
so is that it really has been transformed.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.240
This is Elizabeth Economy. She is a senior fellow&nbsp;
and director for Asia studies here at the Council.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.220
She is also a distinguished visiting fellow&nbsp;
at Stanford University's Hoover Institution.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.720
ECONOMY: You know, beginning in about&nbsp;
2008, with the global financial crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.660
China's, you know, hosting the Olympics. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:09.300
really moments that defined in the minds of&nbsp;
many Chinese leaders that China was rising.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.500
Douglas Paal: The Chinese have many&nbsp;
goals for these Olympics one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.691
was to announce to the world that&nbsp;
China is back after 200 bad years.

00:03:17.691 --> 00:03:21.540
DW News: China’s economy has grown&nbsp;
faster than that of any other major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.480
country... The Asian giant has now grown into one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.845
of the most important export markets for&nbsp;
manufacturers from all over the world.

00:03:28.845 --> 00:03:31.440
Mark Schwartz: This is a period&nbsp;
of historic change in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.480
There haven’t been many periods&nbsp;
in history as fascinating as this.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.620
ECONOMY: So there was a real sense within&nbsp;
China for the first time that they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.700
always expected that at some point, China&nbsp;
was going to surpass the United States. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.100
maybe that time was coming sooner than they had&nbsp;
anticipated. But what really has changed the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.900
on the ground has been Xi Jinping.&nbsp;
Everything for Xi Jinping is under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.700
sort of mantra of the great rejuvenation&nbsp;
of the Chinese nation. And it is a call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.760
for reclaiming a much greater degree of&nbsp;
centrality for China on the global stage.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.500
SIERRA: Xi Jinping became China’s&nbsp;
president in 2013. Some observers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.980
have called him the most powerful&nbsp;
Chinese leader since Mao Zedong.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.180
ECONOMY: Look, I think there&nbsp;
are any number of objectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.060
and we can find them all in Xi Jinping's writings&nbsp;
and speeches. But fundamentally, what I think Xi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.200
Jinping is attempting to do, is simply to&nbsp;
make the world safe for authoritarianism.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.600
Jessica CHEN WEISS:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.020
Xi Jinping is a dictator, but dictators still&nbsp;
have to answer to domestic constituents.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:44.760
SIERRA: This is Jessica Chen Weiss.&nbsp;
Associate Professor of Government at Cornell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.680
and a leading expert on Chinese politics. She&nbsp;
has a different take on China’s expansion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.168
one that sees it as being less&nbsp;
offensive and more defensive.

00:04:54.168 --> 00:05:00.120
CHEN WEISS: China is concerned about a whole&nbsp;
lot of different risks. Some of them domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.680
and others, ones that sort of emanate from&nbsp;
abroad and you know, sparks that might,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.960
you know, start the prairie fire and bring&nbsp;
down the Chinese government. And my take is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.340
that, you know, it's overriding purpose&nbsp;
is to continue to make a world safe for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.120
the Chinese Communist Party to survive&nbsp;
at home. So this is a world that's safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:25.020
for autocracy to coexist alongside democracy&nbsp;
in the international space. So it's not been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:31.080
as ideological I think in its foreign&nbsp;
policy as some have made it out to be.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:35.460
SIERRA: So China is trying to find&nbsp;
a way to sort of fit in with a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.980
that might not be comfortable&nbsp;
with its model of government?

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:45.300
CHEN WEISS: Yeah. It's tried to make space for&nbsp;
its form of government to be regarded as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:51.540
that can continue to exist that is legitimate.&nbsp;
That democracy isn't the only form of government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:57.080
so to speak. And so this has made it easier&nbsp;
for other authoritarian states to survive.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:02.700
SIERRA: Whether offensive or defensive,&nbsp;
evidence of China’s expansion can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:08.340
read about every day in the news. And if&nbsp;
China is exporting authoritarianism then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.900
a big part of how they’re doing it is through&nbsp;
something called the Belt and Road Initiative.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.140
PBS NewsHour: China’s Belt&nbsp;
and Road Initiative is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.840
most expensive infrastructure project in history.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.280
PBS NewsHour: 2,000 years ago the ancient&nbsp;
silk road helped China spread goods, ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.720
and culture all the way to Europe.&nbsp;
Today, China aspires to recreate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.520
maritime silk road of ports and an&nbsp;
economic belt across 70 countries.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:40.500
ECONOMY: So, Belt and Road Initiative is really&nbsp;
Xi Jinping's calling card. And at the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.480
it was really an effort to export&nbsp;
overcapacity, right? China had kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.920
built up its own infrastructure, to the point&nbsp;
where there wasn't that much left to do. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.240
it had big cement industries and hydropower&nbsp;
companies and, you know, lots of coal-fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.280
power plant knowledge and it wanted to export&nbsp;
that. Second, he wanted to connect these poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.660
parts of the country to external markets. The&nbsp;
coastal part of the country is really well off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.520
but much of the interior parts of the country&nbsp;
are very poor. So this was an opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:14.820
look westward, to extend China's reach, first in&nbsp;
terms of hard infrastructure, ports, pipelines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:21.120
you know, railroads, highways, then the digital&nbsp;
Belt and Road, so E-commerce, satellite systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:28.620
fiber optic cables. Initially maybe 60 countries,&nbsp;
you know, through Asia, the Middle East, Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:34.560
and Africa, but now through the entire world.&nbsp;
The investment is actually smaller than many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.660
people expect. You kind of hear this number&nbsp;
of a trillion dollars in Chinese investment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:45.000
How are we ever gonna compete? The reality is&nbsp;
that investment levels are closer to $12 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:51.480
$15 billion on an annual basis over the past, say&nbsp;
six years. What China really does is lend money.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.720
CBC News - The National: You’re&nbsp;
saying China essentially loaned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.560
money to itself to do work in another&nbsp;
country - Well, that is what it did.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.660
PBS NewsHour: Countries can lose sovereignty&nbsp;
and China and gain assets. Sri Lanka had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.140
to hand over a port when it couldn’t&nbsp;
afford debt payments to a Chinese bank.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.480
ECONOMY:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:15.720
It lends buckets of money to countries. And it&nbsp;
expects that those countries when it lends that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:21.000
money will use Chinese labor, will use Chinese&nbsp;
products. So this is a great deal for China.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.791
SIERRA: Right. I was gonna&nbsp;
say, what's the trade-off here?

00:08:22.791 --> 00:08:27.060
ECONOMY: Right. It’s win-win, China&nbsp;
wins twice. So if you look at pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:31.260
much any Belt and Road country, any country&nbsp;
that's been hosting Belt and Road projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:35.760
you will find that there are some fairly&nbsp;
significant protests on the ground. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:41.100
even among China's closest friends like Pakistan&nbsp;
and Cambodia, you will see that the people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.620
protesting these projects. You know why? Because&nbsp;
China does bring in its own labor for many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.300
these projects. So the people themselves aren't&nbsp;
realizing the benefits. There's also the export&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:56.100
of China's way of doing business. So there's a&nbsp;
lack of transparency in the tendering process for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:01.680
these deals. You know, there are environmental and&nbsp;
social ramifications are not really accounted for.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.000
CHEN WEISS: The belt and road&nbsp;
initiative is one of Xi Jinping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.280
signature international initiatives. I&nbsp;
tend to side with those that see this as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.440
more of a bumper sticker slogan. One&nbsp;
that Xi Jinping is proud to hold up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:23.040
but there is a lot of diversity in terms of&nbsp;
the different projects that make up the belt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:29.400
and road. And it isn't one sort of single grand&nbsp;
strategy that we can easily put our finger on.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:37.500
SIERRA: So it seems like a lot of this&nbsp;
comes down to motives and goals. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.180
can we really know what China or&nbsp;
Xi Jinping's ultimate motives are?

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:49.020
ECONOMY: It's actually really easy to know what&nbsp;
Xi Jinping's motives are because he has published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:56.340
already two books of his own speeches, and it is&nbsp;
very clear what he desires. And it's all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:03.420
this great rejuvenation of the Chinese nation.&nbsp;
He's very ambitious. He talks about China leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:08.700
the reform of the global governance system. He&nbsp;
talks about the necessity of the dissolution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:16.320
the US-led alliance system. There is really,&nbsp;
no hidden, you know, meaning or purpose to Xi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.020
Jinping. He is very upfront about where he sees&nbsp;
China in the future. Where he wants China to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:27.780
in the future, the place that he wants China to&nbsp;
hold, on the global stage. So I don't think we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:33.780
need to be trying to divine motives. I think he's&nbsp;
very, very clear about what those motives are.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:39.480
SIERRA: The United States has also tried to&nbsp;
shape the way other countries develop and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:44.640
govern in the past. So how are China's&nbsp;
methods and goals different from ours?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.960
ECONOMY: Well, in many respects, what&nbsp;
China is doing, is exactly the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.720
I mean, the effort to train officials, to&nbsp;
provide guidance on laws and regulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:01.740
to help support through technology the system&nbsp;
of governance that you are, helping to develop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:06.240
all of these things are the exact same things&nbsp;
that the United States does. Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.700
I just tend to think that, you know, we're&nbsp;
promoting democracy at least ostensibly. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:18.240
they're promoting a system of authoritarianism.&nbsp;
So I don't actually see that much difference in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.760
the methods that we use except when we look to&nbsp;
the really coercive methods, but in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:29.180
capacity building, we're doing many of the same&nbsp;
things just with different ultimate objectives.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:35.520
SIERRA: So, what exactly is China’s model&nbsp;
of government? Do some Googling and you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:41.820
see a whole bunch of phrases. From a unitary,&nbsp;
one-party, socialist republic to a Communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:47.720
party-led state - there’s no shortage of debate.&nbsp;
But Liz has a simpler way of describing it.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:54.240
ECONOMY: I think a good way of capturing the&nbsp;
Chinese governance system is to say that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:00.240
basically a system of authoritarian capitalism.&nbsp;
It is a variant of that. Yes, there is a Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.980
Communist Party, which you won't find in any&nbsp;
other country, of course, but at its heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:11.580
it really is just a single-party state, in which&nbsp;
the party exerts an enormous amount of control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:17.280
over society, over the, you know, political&nbsp;
system, over the media, over the internet.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:22.440
SIERRA: So is China exporting&nbsp;
this model of government?

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:28.740
CHEN WEISS: I don't see China as exporting this&nbsp;
model of government. There is some emulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.440
that may be going on, by countries that look&nbsp;
to China’s example admiringly and want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:41.760
copy elements of it. And there's also some&nbsp;
diffusion going on where China is literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.800
selling some of the technologies that, including&nbsp;
surveillance technology, that China has developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:53.880
to monitor its citizens at home and is happy&nbsp;
to sell that overseas for profit. China is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.480
you know, seeking to go places where it serves&nbsp;
its economic and political interests. China tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.980
to work with whatever countries, whatever&nbsp;
forms of government are that are out there.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:09.480
ECONOMY: Well, this, of course, is&nbsp;
a big debate. But I come down pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.360
clearly on the side of - yes, in fact, they are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:20.760
It's not about exporting the Chinese Communist&nbsp;
Party, but it is about exporting elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:24.540
this authoritarian capitalist system, you&nbsp;
know, very much in the same way that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:30.960
United States and other market democracies,&nbsp;
export, elements, of their system, so too is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:38.160
China now exporting elements, doing training&nbsp;
activities, and other things, that support the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:45.180
development of authoritarian capitalist systems.
 
SIERRA: One way to start unpacking the debate is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:49.860
to take a look at the two basic ways that&nbsp;
China is helping other nations govern their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:55.140
citizens. The first is by literally sending&nbsp;
them to school. Officials and legislators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:59.820
from around the world are traveling to China&nbsp;
to attend trainings where they can learn from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:05.840
China’s experience. Officials from at least 36&nbsp;
countries have attended these trainings so far.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:12.660
ECONOMY: It's about helping them develop and&nbsp;
pass laws and regulations, that again, support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:19.080
this kind of authoritarian model. Right? So&nbsp;
you're going to have internet laws that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:24.240
designed to constrain opportunities for the&nbsp;
free flow of information. How do you manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.620
civil society? How do you control the media?&nbsp;
There are all sorts of training opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:31.620
but there are also other things. Training&nbsp;
opportunities for poverty alleviation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:37.260
other elements of the China model, developing&nbsp;
infrastructure, you know, the heavy role of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:42.240
state in state-owned enterprises. How do&nbsp;
you develop those? How do you use them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:47.760
advance your economic and political interests?&nbsp;
Beyond that, you have training that is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:52.320
like training for the Justice ministers or&nbsp;
economic ministers or defense ministers.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:57.000
SIERRA: The other broad element is that&nbsp;
China, and private Chinese companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:02.760
are providing tech to monitor, censor, and&nbsp;
control citizens. The most well-known part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:07.080
of this is called “safe city technology”,&nbsp;
you know, the tech that really hooked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:11.880
president of Kazakhstan. It’s a mixture&nbsp;
of tried-and-true security cameras and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:16.740
cutting edge facial recognition that has made&nbsp;
China the global capital of surveillance.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:21.350
Wall Street Journal: To be in Xinjiang means&nbsp;
being checked every day, multiple times a day.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:24.300
BBC News: China’s sophisticated and controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:29.021
data surveillance system is used&nbsp;
to track citizens on and off-line.

00:15:29.021 --> 00:15:32.160
ABC News In-depth: A personal scorecard for 1.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:36.817
billion citizens - rewards for good&nbsp;
behavior and punishments for bad.

00:15:36.817 --> 00:15:41.580
FRANCE 24 English: The QR codes have now become&nbsp;
the passports that allow Chinese people to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:46.380
around, to get into a shop, one must show&nbsp;
a green health code proving good health. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:54.000
CHEN WEISS: So one of the areas that China&nbsp;
has been kind of a leader is in this sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:00.780
AI-powered surveillance. It's something that China&nbsp;
has developed extensively to use at home. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:06.840
that's been particularly on display in Xinjiang,&nbsp;
where China has, you know, interned more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:12.240
a million Uyghurs and other ethnic minorities&nbsp;
and what many have called concentration camps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:18.780
But again, China is not the only purveyor&nbsp;
of safe city technology. And in other cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:23.760
safe cities are seen as, you know,&nbsp;
effective ways to reduce crime as well.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:27.660
ECONOMY: So in terms of the&nbsp;
whole safe city initiative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.300
there are a couple of things to&nbsp;
bear in mind. First, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:35.280
the idea behind safe cities I think could be&nbsp;
attractive to any country. Right? What police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:42.420
department wouldn't be pleased with the idea&nbsp;
that you could catch a crime in progress or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:47.640
identify a criminal within, you know, minutes&nbsp;
of that person having committed some crime?

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:48.000
SIERRA: Right.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:52.080
ECONOMY: That is attractive. Right? So&nbsp;
what we're really talking about is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:57.660
far the system can be taken and how it&nbsp;
can be turned into an abuse of people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:03.180
rights. And I think here's where everything&nbsp;
starts to become a little more concerning.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:11.580
Huawei for example, which is a major provider&nbsp;
of safe city technology, has said that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:18.240
providing this technology, you know, in over 200&nbsp;
cities globally. But what it might include would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:25.320
be the television cameras, with the closed circuit&nbsp;
TVs, the control centers, the data processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:31.320
and then other things that might be included&nbsp;
will be things like drones. And then Huawei will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:36.480
sometimes provide the training, for example,&nbsp;
in Uganda. They have trained almost 1,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:45.120
Ugandans on how to do E-governance and how to&nbsp;
manage the sort of safe city technology. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.680
for countries that have authoritarian&nbsp;
leanings, you know, the opportunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:54.412
to be able to have that level of detail&nbsp;
on every single one of your citizens -

00:17:54.412 --> 00:17:54.720
SIERRA: Right.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:57.540
ECONOMY: To know, you know, with&nbsp;
whom people are meeting, and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:01.200
are they participating in a protest? Are&nbsp;
they putting things on the internet that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:05.100
are anti-government in some way? This is&nbsp;
very attractive to governments like that.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:10.860
CHEN WEISS: Most countries around the world&nbsp;
use some kind of surveillance technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:16.320
and that China is only one of many suppliers.&nbsp;
And many countries rely on multiple different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:22.860
suppliers to source this technology. So, you know,&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia for example, uses technology from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:29.880
China to surveil its citizens. But they also are&nbsp;
buying technology from UK and US-based companies.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:34.620
SIERRA: So why do you think it is that the&nbsp;
Chinese technology is what people are questioning?

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:43.260
CHEN WEISS: It’s an important question. I think&nbsp;
it's easier to say that the problems that arise in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:49.080
the world stem from things that other bad actors&nbsp;
are doing. It's harder to look within ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:55.440
and recognize that we are struggling with many&nbsp;
of the same problems including ubiquitous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:01.080
digital surveillance. Difficulty figuring out&nbsp;
what's the appropriate model for regulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:06.380
the internet, sort of respecting privacy, but&nbsp;
also the kind of the free flow of information.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:13.500
SIERRA: The combination of know-how&nbsp;
and equipment seems to be the footing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:18.240
for tech-driven authoritarianism, and&nbsp;
China has found willing partners in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.720
authoritarian regimes across&nbsp;
the world. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:27.660
China is seeking to use its clout to shift the&nbsp;
conversation even in powerful Democratic nations.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:33.840
ECONOMY: A second is really exporting the China&nbsp;
model through, international institutions, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:41.280
So trying to make sure that Chinese values around&nbsp;
things like human rights, or internet sovereignty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:47.580
or even its positions on things like Taiwan or the&nbsp;
South China Sea, are reflected in international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:52.320
institutions. Right? You know, the United Nations,&nbsp;
so you can see over the past several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:57.180
they've become very active in trying to&nbsp;
change the language around human rights.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:20:03.360
To be more explicit, you know, we recognize human&nbsp;
rights as inalienable, right? We are all born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:10.260
with our inalienable rights. China says rights&nbsp;
are granted and can be taken away by the state.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:11.040
SIERRA: That's different.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.720
ECONOMY: Right? Exactly. It's very different.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:17.520
ECONOMY: And inalienable rights means the right&nbsp;
to free speech. Right? Means you're gonna have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:23.040
things like the free media, the right to protest.&nbsp;
These are all things that are antithetical to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:27.600
China model, to an authoritarian political&nbsp;
system, which is about constraining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:32.700
rights of individuals to act independently.&nbsp;
Beyond that China is looking for support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:37.680
on the global stage. So when you know, the&nbsp;
United States and other countries, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:43.500
in Western Europe, Australia, tried to put forward&nbsp;
a resolution condemning Chinese detention of, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:48.180
know, a million and a half Uyghurs in labor and&nbsp;
re-education camps, in Xinjiang Autonomous Region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:55.940
China managed to get upwards of 50 countries to&nbsp;
say that China's human rights practices are great.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:21:01.800
SIERRA: This international support is even&nbsp;
more shocking when you take a closer look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:07.740
China’s record. Global rights watchdogs cite a&nbsp;
broad increase in violations under Xi Jinping.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:12.720
The most obvious case is the government’s&nbsp;
persecution of Muslims in Xinjiang, where many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:17.760
Islamic practices have been banned, and where&nbsp;
at least one million people are being held in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:23.880
political re-education camps. Then there’s China’s&nbsp;
unprecedented system of mass surveillance, and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:29.100
strict repression of free speech, both online and&nbsp;
in political hot spots like Tibet and Hong Kong.

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:35.100
Those who advocate for human rights in China face&nbsp;
imprisonment and enforced disappearance, as do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:41.160
advocates for women and LGBTQ communities. Human&nbsp;
Rights Watch says that China’s ability to erode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:46.800
international norms, represents an “existential&nbsp;
threat to the rights of people worldwide.”

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:55.560
ECONOMY: So I think, you know, when it comes&nbsp;
to dealing with criticism from countries about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.560 --> 00:22:00.960
Chinese behavior, the Chinese government very much&nbsp;
looks to these countries that it is supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.820
through Belt and Road projects and in other ways,&nbsp;
to stand up behind it and give it its support.

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:08.400
SIERRA: Right. So friends who will&nbsp;
vote for you when the time comes.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:12.420
ECONOMY: Absolutely. And frankly, just look&nbsp;
at what happened with Coronavirus. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:21.720
you saw Pakistan, Cambodia, Nigeria, all these&nbsp;
countries refuse to repatriate their own citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:29.520
from Wuhan. Right, which was the epicenter of this&nbsp;
Coronavirus as a statement of their friendship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:37.260
China. So I think that's the type of, sort of&nbsp;
reward, that China really values, as coming out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:41.820
of these sorts of, you know, capacity building&nbsp;
and economic projects that it's been doing.

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:44.160
SIERRA: Right. And that's not&nbsp;
even long term. That's right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:45.960
they're already seeing the result of that.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:50.880
ECONOMY: Oh, absolutely. So that's the&nbsp;
second element of China's export of its,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:54.600
of its model. And then the third is a little bit&nbsp;
different. Because when you think about export,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:58.800
of course, you think about having a willing&nbsp;
consumer. Right? You have somebody who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:05.820
importing it on the other end. But in some&nbsp;
cases, China uses the leverage of its market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:11.400
right? Because it has a huge market to&nbsp;
try to coerce other countries or actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:16.800
to accept it's model or elements of its model.&nbsp;
And I think a really great example of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.960
was here in the United States, with the case of&nbsp;
the Houston Rockets General Manager, Daryl Morey.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:24.120
Trevor Noah: General manager of the Houston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:28.980
Rockets posted a tweet in support of&nbsp;
the pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:33.240
CNN: Despite his apologies, several Chinese&nbsp;
businesses are now suspending ties with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:37.680
Houston Rockets over that tweet. The NBA’s&nbsp;
response is also raising questions over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:41.100
lengths that businesses in the US&nbsp;
have to go, and are willing to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:45.660
to stay in favor with the Chinese government&nbsp;
for access to the lucrative Chinese market.

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:51.240
ECONOMY: And when he tweeted, you know,&nbsp;
“Fight for freedom stand with Hong Kong.”

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:51.600
SIERRA: Right.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:56.220
ECONOMY: You know, China responded by, you know,&nbsp;
canceling all the broadcasts of the NBA games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:00.960
all of the Houston Rockets licensing deals&nbsp;
were pulled for, you know, their merchandising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:07.680
in China. And what came out of that afterward was&nbsp;
not just simply the effort by China to use, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:13.500
it's market to pressure the NBA. But a statement&nbsp;
in the People's Daily that said, essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:18.780
that when people are talking about things related&nbsp;
to sovereignty or social stability, these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:25.140
do not fall within the purview of free speech. So&nbsp;
imagine that here's this government in China, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:31.140
is trying to tell people in the United States and&nbsp;
elsewhere, right? There's a whole range of issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:37.620
that you in the United States and other countries&nbsp;
cannot discuss publicly because they offend us.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:38.100
SIERRA: Right.

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:43.620
ECONOMY: So it's a very subtle and coercive&nbsp;
way, really, of exporting their models, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:47.047
they're basically saying - you can't talk about&nbsp;
Taiwan, Hong Kong or anything else we decide -

00:24:47.047 --> 00:24:47.340
SIERRA: Right.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.880
ECONOMY: You know, relates&nbsp;
to our social stability.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:55.980
SIERRA: So, China’s newfound global economic&nbsp;
power has succeeded in getting dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.980 --> 00:25:00.960
countries to vote in its favor at the UN.&nbsp;
And it has also pushed democratic nations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:05.540
to concede to censorship in order to&nbsp;
gain access to its enormous market.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:10.380
SIERRA: What do you think that&nbsp;
China wants to get out of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:12.720
efforts in the long term? What's the goal here?

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:18.300
CHEN WEISS: Well ultimately, as I said, I&nbsp;
think, you know, China wants to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:25.740
strong and resilient global power. But China also&nbsp;
is, you know, very much, sort of going where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:30.840
opportunities present themselves. Where,&nbsp;
you know, is Chinese assistance in demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:37.040
and where there's an opportunity, you know,&nbsp;
left by, the lack of, competitive alternatives.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:40.260
SIERRA: So, I mean, do you&nbsp;
see this as just sort of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:42.000
good business opportunity that they're taking?

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:45.900
CHEN WEISS: Largely I do see&nbsp;
it that way. It's partly that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:51.480
it’s also I think an effort to secure&nbsp;
access to resources in the event of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:56.700
global disruption. Markets, of course, are&nbsp;
important, but, you know, China, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:01.620
recognizes that they can't always be relied upon&nbsp;
in the event of conflict. And so, you know, China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:06.020
has also invested strategically,&nbsp;
in developing those relationships.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:13.740
ECONOMY: So I am quite concerned about the&nbsp;
ability of China to export its own values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:20.060
its own conception of what is allowed&nbsp;
under free speech into the United States.

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:25.980
SIERRA: Do you think that China's efforts&nbsp;
are already changing norms around the world?

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:32.100
ECONOMY: So, I think there's no doubt that China's&nbsp;
already transforming international norms. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:37.560
see it when countries are supporting China,&nbsp;
when China rejects rulings by the Permanent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:43.440
Court of Arbitration in the South China Sea. And&nbsp;
it has scores of countries stand up for it there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:49.920
or it has, many countries stand up, behind its&nbsp;
behavior in Xinjiang autonomous region. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:55.440
the Belt and Road Initiative has now been&nbsp;
put into more than 20 different UN agencies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.440 --> 00:27:01.800
Right? So you have UN officials, traveling to&nbsp;
Africa and saying you should participate in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:07.560
Belt and Road. I think there are many respects&nbsp;
in which China already is changing the discourse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:15.480
and is finding ways to ensure that international&nbsp;
institutions and other countries are accepting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:22.080
its norms, its values, its policy priorities,&nbsp;
simply because it is such a big economic player.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:26.760
SIERRA: Are you worried that all of&nbsp;
this playing defense against China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:28.940
will lead to escalating tensions?

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:34.980
ECONOMY: Frankly speaking, yes, it's entirely&nbsp;
possible that we'll have escalating tensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:42.000
we already have escalating tensions. My hope is&nbsp;
that by pushing back, that what we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:48.660
do is crack things open, so that we can actually&nbsp;
have negotiations, right? So by going out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:53.160
with sort of this higher bar, in terms of&nbsp;
the standards for infrastructure projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:59.640
that that will actually entice China to raise&nbsp;
its standards. It's not about preventing China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:05.040
from doing infrastructure globally. It's&nbsp;
about ensuring that the infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:10.680
that is developed is done at the highest&nbsp;
standards. Right? You want fair competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:15.840
right? For multinationals to participate. But&nbsp;
you also want the environmental standards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:20.400
the labor standards to be ones that serve the&nbsp;
interests of the people in the host countries.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:26.040
SIERRA: What do you think the risks&nbsp;
are of exaggerating the threat that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:29.100
China poses to the US’s place in the world?

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:36.120
CHEN WEISS: I think there are two big risks of&nbsp;
exaggerating the danger that China poses. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:45.660
is that it is likely to turn the US-China&nbsp;
competition into one that's life or death by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:52.500
convincing Chinese even more than they are already&nbsp;
that there is no place for China to lead in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:59.160
world. And that second, it is dangerous because&nbsp;
it's more likely to isolate the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:03.000
There are very few countries&nbsp;
around the world that want to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:08.460
a choice between China and the United States. And&nbsp;
they aren't likely to take sides. And if they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.520
we, in the United States may or&nbsp;
may not come out ahead in that.

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:16.020
SIERRA: So it could become a self-fulfilling&nbsp;
prophecy if we keep pushing that narrative?

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:21.420
CHEN WEISS: Right. The United States competition&nbsp;
with China does not need to be a cold war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:26.880
but I think the policies of, you know,&nbsp;
existential competition, if framed that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:31.320
may still have the counterproductive&nbsp;
effect of actually bringing one about.

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:38.040
SIERRA: What do you say to people who will say&nbsp;
that China isn't necessarily playing offense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:40.820
they're just trying to make the world&nbsp;
safe for their system of government?

00:29:40.820 --> 00:29:45.780
ECONOMY: I would say that it is a&nbsp;
distinction without a difference that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:51.120
the best defense is a good offense, that&nbsp;
Xi Jinping has talked very directly about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:57.420
the need for China to lead in the reform of the&nbsp;
global governance system. And that if protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:03.540
China means that China is going to change norms&nbsp;
around human rights, around internet governance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:08.880
around freedom of navigation, around free&nbsp;
trade, around standard setting, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:14.460
for infrastructure projects globally, that&nbsp;
that to me fundamentally is not simply about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:20.700
protecting China's interests. It is about&nbsp;
changing the norms of the rest of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:25.740
to align with those of China. And that&nbsp;
is fundamentally an offensive strategy.

